## Soviet preparations
A Deputy Director of the Soviet Defense Ministry's Institute of Military Affairs, Yuri Kirshin, documented that the Red Army had around 23,000 tanks available at the start of Barbarossa of which 14,700 were combat-ready.[[170]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Barbarossa#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKirshin1997385-189) Around 11,000 tanks were in the western military districts that faced the German invasion force.[[11]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Barbarossa#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMercatante201264-14) According to military historian John Burtt, 17,520 tanks out of a total of Soviet 24,000 were out of commission while undergoing maintenance in June 1941, thereby leaving only 6,480 available for frontline service at the time of the German invasion.[[171]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Barbarossa#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBurtt20166-190)_[better source needed_]
Hitler later declared to some of his generals, "If I had known about the Russian tank strength in 1941 I would not have attacked".[[172]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Barbarossa#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMacksey1989456-191) However, maintenance and readiness standards were very poor;
